LATEST IMPLEMENTATION — MB-03C
AppendRecoveryRequestV2::new(parent, candidate, exact_block) owns independently
retained AppendCheckpointV2 endpoints and the exact intended BlockV2. Private
fields and immutable parent()/candidate()/block() accessors prevent mutation.
The constructor bounds parent/candidate height and generation, requires adjacent
height/generation and same network, and checks the child header against both pins.
Construction does not establish actual block validity.

AppendStoreV2::recover_interrupted(root, identity, &request, action) acquires
exclusive ownership of an EXISTING append store. Consuming store.recover(&request,
action) retains the SAME writer lock, even from a fenced writer; its last confirmed
memory checkpoint must match one request endpoint. A read-only handle cannot
recover. Every failure returns no handle; no drop/reacquire gap, raw descriptor,
mutable chain access or PID takeover exists.

The complete old prefix is loaded from disk using independently chosen network and
parent pins. The exact proposed child is accepted through ChainV2, including work,
signatures, order, account/membership execution, roots and cumulative budgets.
Both exact canonical MSU2 selectors are derived from accepted histories; checksum-
correct but wrong manifest or byte-total metadata still rejects. Full candidate
validation is also required for RetainParent before any deletion.

SUPPORTED RECOVERY STATES
The root contains exactly manifest.v2, blocks/, selection.v2 and, when present,
selection.pending. Every complete parent block uses its canonical filename: a
20-digit height followed by .mrb2. Only the next-height residual may be additional.
Here OLD means the exact parent selector and NEW means the exact candidate selector.

- OLD with no tail or pending selector: Complete explicitly resumes the retained
  candidate append; RetainParent resynchronizes the old selection.
- OLD with an empty, partial-prefix or complete exact child and no pending selector:
  Complete writes only the missing canonical suffix and publishes it. RetainParent
  removes that authorized unselected child and retains the old selection.
- OLD with the complete exact child and an empty, partial-prefix or complete exact
  candidate selector: Complete finishes and publishes the selector. RetainParent
  removes the pending selector, then the child, in the synchronized order below.
- NEW with the complete exact child and no pending selector: Complete replays and
  resynchronizes idempotently without advancing the generation again. RetainParent
  rejects; it cannot roll back a selected candidate.
- A pending selector with a missing or partial child rejects. NEW with a missing
  or partial child, or with any pending selector, also rejects. Foreign bytes,
  non-prefix or trailing data, unknown names, extra heights, wrong pins, unsafe
  aliases, and missing or corrupt selected history are preserved and rejected.

Interrupted initialization and arbitrary corruption repair remain unsupported.

The request is the caller's narrow authorization for these paths. Empty/common
prefixes do not prove who created files; prefix equality is a byte constraint,
not historical or cryptographic provenance. The component does not persist the
request or choose authority from the damaged record, peer or highest generation.
No journal, intent file, storage profile, hash domain or wire encoding was added.

Before mutation, recovery resynchronizes validated parent manifest/history,
directories and current marker. Complete opens a safe current regular file,
rechecks observed prefix and descriptor/path identity, appends only the missing
suffix, verifies the complete bytes, and synchronizes the block and blocks/
directory. It does the same for the pending selector, rechecks the old marker and
artifacts, renames the pending selector over selection.v2, and synchronizes the
stable root. It never truncates a mismatched file.

RetainParent works only while OLD is selected. It compares the old marker and
known artifact bytes before deletion. It removes the matching pending selector
and synchronizes the root BEFORE removing the matching tail and synchronizing
blocks/. This preserves recognized states for a retry. It never deletes an unknown
artifact or a selected block. Final success for either action requires the existing
strict clean loader, equality with the complete expected ChainV2, ledger and resource
counters, and file/directory resynchronization.

RecoveryIncomplete does not claim that cleanup or preparation persisted.
PublicationUncertain applies once publication was attempted or NEW was already
selected at entry.
Retry uses the same independently authorized request and revalidates actual disk
state. All normal open_writer/inspect/clean-state reconcile entry points still
reject staging. There is no automatic cleanup, repair, migration, global freshness,
branch choice or state-cache shortcut.

RETAINED STORAGE FOUNDATION
StoreV2 profile 1 is immutable. Its exact 232-byte MSS2 record uses encoding
version 2, storage profile 1 and generation zero. AppendStoreV2 profile 2 uses the
exact 280-byte MSU2 envelope: a 16-byte wrapper containing magic, version 2,
profile 2 and a u64 generation; the unchanged 232-byte MSS2 snapshot; and a SHA256d
checksum over MIRROR_STORAGE_APPEND_SELECTION_V2 followed by the first 248 bytes.
Embedded MSS2 retains profile 1 and generation zero. MNM2/MRB2 bytes and all prior
fixtures and hash domains remain unchanged.

Initialization is explicit in a new or empty root using an already accepted
ChainV2. Generation zero may start at a nonzero accepted height. Each published
child advances height and generation once. Generation cannot exceed height and
is not branch weight. Normal opening requires independent ManifestIdentityV2 and
AppendCheckpointV2 values, followed by complete actual-chain replay.

Successful append synchronizes before advancing memory. Disk discrepancies or
mutation failures fence chain(), selection() and checkpoint() access.
last_confirmed_checkpoint describes prior successful memory, not current disk
authority. Consuming clean-state reconcile retains the same lock and returns an
unfenced writer only after complete selected replay and resynchronization.
Inspection holds a shared lock and performs no application write, synchronization
or repair.

OWNERSHIP, BOUNDS AND ASSUMPTIONS
Cooperative nonblocking Linux locks attach to the stable root inode. A writer
holds an exclusive lock; inspectors hold shared locks for their handle lifetimes.
There is no root swap, clone, raw lock handle or stale-lock cleanup. The lock is
released when the last descriptor sharing its open file description closes.
Inherited fork descriptors may prolong ownership. Keep the parent process-test
mutex isolation; do not hide ownership failures with sleeps or retries.

Local owners and parent directories are trusted. Correct operating-system locking,
atomic rename, and file/directory synchronization are assumptions. Static alias,
file-type and current-identity checks are not protection against a hostile same-user
process or proof of historical inode provenance. Verification covers recorded local
Linux filesystems and Ubuntu CI. WSL2 native storage is intended but not directly
verified. DrvFS, native Windows, NFS and SMB remain unverified.

Chain caps remain 4,096 blocks including genesis, 16 MiB of complete MRB2 bytes and
1,048,576 cumulative abstract state/configuration items. The MNM2 cap is 2,781,636
bytes. MSS2 and MSU2 selectors are 232 and 280 bytes respectively. The profile-2
clean selected encoded maximum is 19,559,132 bytes. Recovery's separate distinct
encoded-input maximum is 19,559,412 bytes, including the full intended child and
both selectors.

Every payload read, comparison and final replay is conservatively charged,
including one-byte sentinels, against MAX_APPEND_RECOVERY_READ_BYTES_V2, which is
156,508,096 bytes per call. The existing strict loader receives an upfront charge
for its maximum read phase; its two marker comparisons are charged separately.
Metadata counts are bounded independently. These are not resident-memory or total
write-volume limits. There is no unbounded scan, retry, pruning, history eviction,
cached-counter authority or silent cap increase.

